    "Housing Search - International Welcome e-desk - University of Strasbourg\nShare the page :\nAdd the page to your favorites :\nPage has been added to favorites\nPage has been removed from favorites\nHousing Search\nThere are several sources of information you can use when looking for a home:\nunistra-internationalhousing.studapart.com\nthe housing partner of the University of Strasbourg;\nPublic (Crous) and private university residencies\n;\nRental agencies\n;\nAds\n;\nthe University of Strasbourg's Housing Guide\nfor students and researchers.\nFor an effective search, you need to\nunderstand the different types of housing in France\n.\nStay in Strasbourg and its surroundings with Studapart, the housing partner of the University of Strasbourg.\nDiscover exclusive housing options near your campus, including studios, shared apartments, university residences, and rooms in private homes. Enjoy personalized support throughout your rental search at unistra-internationalhousing.studapart.com!\nComplete all your rental steps through the platform: from submitting your rental application and booking your accomodation to subscribing to home insurance and accessing additional services offered by Studapart.\nPlease note that Studapart is an external partner of the University of Strasbourg. Users interested in the services provided by this partner should read through its terms of use and data protection policy before registering.\nunistra-internationalhousing.studapart.com\nIf you are an international exchange student,\nyou must submit your application for CROUs housing as part of your application\nfor an exchange program at the University of Strasbourg.\nIf you are not an international exchange student, the public university housing offices (Crous)\noffer students receiving financial aid\nhousing options (standard, accessible, and short-term) for a period of 10 to 12 months (from September 1 to August 31). Since there is very high demand, you should begin the application process as early as possible.\nApplying for a spot in a private university residence is similar to renting through an agent or individual. You'll need to put together an application file. Upon moving in, you may need to pay a deposit along with the monthly rent. The deposit is refundable when you leave if the property is clean and undamaged.\nThey offer\naccommodation in the private sector.\nAn agency fee will be charged if your application is accepted (approximately 1 month's rent).\nAdvertisements in newspapers, online, on social networks and in shops gather\naccomodations owned by private individuals\n. You can also find advertisements\nin rental agencies or notaries\n.\nCheck your eligibility for Crous accommodation\nCrous accommodation offers\nWatch out for housing scams!\nIf you have any doubts about an ad or a request from a landlord or rental agent, contact the Bas-Rhin Departmental Housing Agency (ADIL): 5 rue Hannong, 67000 Strasbourg, 03 88 21 07 06, adil67.org.\nYou'll receive free advice on legal, financial and fiscal aspects of housing.\nWebsite of the ADIL - Bas-Rhin\nThe University of Strasbourg Housing Guide is a great resource for finding housing. It offers a variety of housing options to match your profile, stay duration, budget, and preferences.\nUnistra's Housing Guide\nTo help you find accommodation in Strasbourg, the International Relations Office has set up a series of digital meetings called \"Zooms logement\". You will\nfind all the information you need, expert advice (\nGuarantie Visale\n) and the opportunity to ask any questions you may have.\nThese sessions are available in French and English.\nFollow the International Relations Office's Instagram account (\n@internationalunistra\n) and/or the “\nPlan your housing search\n” page to stay updated on the schedule for housing Zoom sessions and see the replay.\n@internationalunistra\nZoom sessions and replay\nPrioritairement dédiée aux doctorants primo-arrivants et en cotutelle, la résidence se situe idéalement sur le campus universitaire Esplanade, Boulevard de la Victoire, tout proche de l’arrêt de tram Observatoire.\nPlus d’informations et réservations au Collège doctoral européen\nLa villa Arconati Visconti est une résidence de l’Université de Strasbourg réservée à l’accueil des professeurs, chercheurs et post-doctorants en court séjour à Strasbourg. La durée de location possible pour un logement est d’une semaine au minimum à trois mois au maximum.\nPlus d’informations et réservations à la Villa Arconati\nIn France, apartments located in buildings are described in property advertisements by their number of rooms (T1, T2...).\nThe smallest apartment is a studio\n. It consists of a single room, which may have several functions (bedroom, kitchen, living room, study), and a bathroom and a WC which may or may not be separate from the bathroom.\nLarger apartments are identified by a letter T (type)\nand a number indicating the number of rooms in the apartment. The kitchen, bathroom and WC are not counted.\nYou will also find\n\"bis\" apartments\n, for example \"T2bis\". This means that one of the 2 rooms in the apartment is large enough to be divided into 2 rooms.\nStudio\n: 1 room with kitchenette + bathroom;\nT1\n: 1 main room + kitchen + bathroom;\nT1 to T2\n: 1 main room + 1 room with kitchenette + bathroom;\nT2\n: 2 rooms + kitchen + bathroom.\nT3\n: 3 rooms (2 bedrooms + 1 living room) + 1 kitchen + 1 bathroom (with separate WC or included)\nT3 bis\n: 3 rooms (2 bedrooms + 1 sitting room), one of which is large enough to be divided into two separate areas + 1 kitchen + 1 bathroom (with separate WC or included).\nMenu\nBrowse those links to navigate the website\nDisplay\nHide\nthe searchbox\nFill in your search :\nLaunch search\nClose\nOur audiences :\nStudent Profile\nResearcher Profile\nI think about it\nBefore\nmy arrival\nI am\nin\nStrasbourg\nBefore\nmy departure\nServices Pack"
